Task 1: Configure an NFS Storage Domain
Subtask 1.1: Prepare the NFS Server
First, we need to set up an NFS server that will provide shared storage for our virtualization environment.

Step 1: Access the NFS Server Node
# Connect to your NFS server node
ssh root@nfs-server-ip
Step 2: Install NFS Server Packages
# Update the system
dnf update -y

# Install NFS utilities
dnf install -y nfs-utils rpcbind
Step 3: Create Storage Directories
# Create directories for different storage domains
mkdir -p /exports/data
mkdir -p /exports/iso
mkdir -p /exports/export

# Set proper ownership and permissions
chown -R 36:36 /exports/data
chown -R 36:36 /exports/iso
chown -R 36:36 /exports/export

chmod -R 755 /exports
Note: The user ID 36 corresponds to the vdsm user in RHVH, which is required for proper access.

Step 4: Configure NFS Exports
# Create NFS exports configuration
cat > /etc/exports << 'EOF'
/exports/data *(rw,sync,no_root_squash,no_all_squash)
/exports/iso *(rw,sync,no_root_squash,no_all_squash)
/exports/export *(rw,sync,no_root_squash,no_all_squash)
EOF
Step 5: Start and Enable NFS Services
# Enable and start rpcbind service
systemctl enable --now rpcbind

# Enable and start NFS server
systemctl enable --now nfs-server

# Export the NFS shares
exportfs -ra

# Verify exports
exportfs -v
Subtask 1.2: Configure Firewall for NFS
Step 1: Configure Firewall Rules
# Add NFS services to firewall
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=nfs
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=rpc-bind
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=mountd

# Add specific ports for NFS
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=2049/tcp
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=2049/udp
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=111/tcp
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-port=111/udp

# Reload firewall configuration
firewall-cmd --reload

# Verify firewall rules
firewall-cmd --list-all
Subtask 1.3: Test NFS Connectivity
Step 1: Test from RHVH Hosts
# From each RHVH host, test NFS connectivity
showmount -e nfs-server-ip

# Expected output should show the exported directories
# Example: /exports/data *
#          /exports/iso *
#          /exports/export *
Task 2: Attach Storage Domains to RHVH Hosts
Subtask 2.1: Access RHVM Web Interface
Step 1: Login to RHVM Manager
Open a web browser and navigate to your RHVM Manager URL
Login with administrator credentials
Navigate to the Storage → Domains section
Subtask 2.2: Create Data Storage Domain
Step 1: Add New Storage Domain
Click New button in the Storage Domains view
Fill in the following details:
General Tab:

Name: nfs-data-domain
Description: Primary data storage domain
Domain Function: Data
Storage Type: NFS
Format: V4 (recommended for new installations)
Storage Details:

Export Path: /exports/data
Server: nfs-server-ip (replace with actual IP)
Advanced Parameters: Leave default values
Step 2: Configure Advanced Options
# Optional: Set mount options for better performance
# In the Advanced Parameters section, you can add:
# Mount Options: vers=4,minorversion=1,proto=tcp
Step 3: Complete Storage Domain Creation
Click OK to create the storage domain
Wait for the domain to be created and activated
Monitor the Events tab for any errors
Subtask 2.3: Create ISO Storage Domain
Step 1: Add ISO Domain
Click New button again
Configure the ISO domain:
General Tab:

Name: nfs-iso-domain
Description: ISO images storage domain
Domain Function: ISO
Storage Type: NFS
Storage Details:

Export Path: /exports/iso
Server: nfs-server-ip
Step 2: Activate ISO Domain
Click OK to create the domain
The ISO domain should automatically attach to the data center
Subtask 2.4: Create Export Storage Domain
Step 1: Add Export Domain
Click New button
Configure the export domain:
General Tab:

Name: nfs-export-domain
Description: VM export storage domain
Domain Function: Export
Storage Type: NFS
Storage Details:

Export Path: /exports/export
Server: nfs-server-ip
